==English==
{{wp|[[Ba'al|+]]}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|Baʿal}}, {{l|en|Ba'al}}
* {{alter|en|Baʻl}}

===Etymology===
{{etymon|en|:der<conj:and>|la-lat:Baal|phn:𐤁𐤏𐤋<tr:bʿl>|uga:𐎁𐎓𐎍<tr:baʿlu>|tree=1}}
From {{der|en|la-lat|Baal}} (as in the [[Vulgate]]) and {{der|en|grc|Βάαλ}}; from {{der|en|he|בַּעַל|tr=bá`al|t=lord, husband, owner}}, {{der|en|phn|𐤁𐤏𐤋|tr=bʿl|t=lord, master<!-- of skill, like 𐤁𐤏𐤋 𐤇𐤓𐤔𐤌 -->, owner}} and {{der|en|uga|𐎁𐎓𐎍|tr=baʿlu|t=lord, owner}}, all from {{der|en|sem-pro|*baʿl-||owner, lord, husband}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈbeɪəl/|/ˈbeɪl/|/ˈbɑːl/|a=UK}}<ref>''Oxford English Dictionary'', 1st ed. "Baal, ''n.'' Oxford University Press (Oxford), 1885.</ref><ref name="lpd">{{cite-book|last=Wells|first=John C.|year=2008|title=Longman Pronunciation Dictionary|edition=3rd|publisher=Longman|isbn=9781405881180}}</ref>
* {{IPA|en|/ˈbeɪəl/|/ˈbeɪl/|/ˈbɑl/|/bɑˈɑl/|a=US}}<ref>''Merriam-Webster Online''. "[http://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/baal baal]". 2015.</ref><ref>''Webb's Easy Bible Names Pronunciation Guide''. "[https://books.google.com.hk/books?id=IrrtAgAAQBAJ&pg=PT36 Baal]". Steven Webb (Riverside), 2012.</ref>
* {{rhymes|en|eɪəl|eɪl|ɑːl|s1=2|s2=1|s3=1,2}}
* {{homophones|en|bael|qq1=UK|;|bail|bale|;|boll|qq4=some US speakers|;|ball|bawl|aa6=cot-caught}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Justinrleung-Baal.wav|a=Canada}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-Baal.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun|~|s|Baalim}}

# {{lb|en|mythology|biblical}} A [[storm]] and [[fertility]] [[god]] of the [[Phoenician]] and [[Canaanite]] [[pantheon]]s, [[reckon]]ed as [[chief]] of the [[god]]s by the 1st [[millennium]] [[BC]].
#* {{RQ:King James Version|1 Kings|18|25|26|passage=25 And Eliiah said vnto the prophets of '''Baal''', Chuse you one bullocke for your selues, and dresse it first, for yee are many: and call on the name of your gods, but put no fire vnder.<br>26 And they took the bullocke which was giuen them, and they dressed it, and called on the name of '''Baal''' from morning, euen vntil noone, saying, O '''Baal''', heare vs. But there was no voyce, nor any that answered: And they leapt vpon the altar which was made.}}
# {{lb|en|mythology|biblical|sometimes|_|lowercase}} [[various|Various]] [[other]] [[Baalim]], [[understood]] as [[distinct]] [[patron]] [[god]]s or as [[local]] [[patron]] [[aspects]] the [[great]] [[god]] Baal.
# {{lb|en|Christianity}} One of the [[demon]]s or [[fallen]] [[angel]]s of [[Satan]].

====Usage notes====
The latinized spelling and anglicized pronunciation is still used for the expanded senses, but modern scholarship increasingly notes the [[ayin]] of the original name by spelling it {{m|en|Baʿal}} or {{m|en|Ba'al}} and pronouncing it more in line with the original Hebrew form. Misunderstood as a solar deity by 19th century scholarship; misunderstood as a collective term for various patron gods by 19th and 20th century scholarship prior to the discovery of inscriptions at [[Ugarit]] showing these to have been understood as aspects of a single divinity, whose worship gradually supplanted that of [[El]]. These aspects are sometimes distinguished by epithets: {{m|en|Baalberith}}, {{m|en|Beelzebub}}, {{m|en|Beelzebul}}, etc.

The Hebraic plural {{m|en|Baalim}} is particularly used for its appearances in the Bible, where it may refer to gods or idols of the god. The anglicized plural is more common in other contexts.

====Derived terms====
{{col|en|Baal-gad|Baalian|Baalic|Baalish|Baalism,Baalist|Baalite|Baalize|Baalzebub}}

====Related terms====
* {{l|en|Bel}}
* {{l|en|Beelzebub}}, {{l|en|Beelzebul}}
* {{l|en|Baal-Shem-Tov}}
*{{l|en|Baal Shem}}

